artifact.self_contained_problem:
Saturn's largest moon Titan is inferred, from measurements of its degree-2 tidal Love number k_2, to host a deep liquid-water ocean beneath an outer ice shell, possibly sandwiched between an outer ice-Ih layer and a layer of high-pressure ice phases (e.g., ice VI) overlying a rocky/hydrated silicate core. The geochemical state of this internal ocean — its content of dissolved organics, salts, redox-active minerals, and biologically relevant nutrients — is poorly constrained. Determine the following:

(a) Whether Titan's deep internal water ocean is habitable, in the sense of containing concentrations of organic compounds, mineral species, and nutrients (sources of C, H, N, O, P, S and metabolically useful redox couples) sufficient to support life as currently understood, or whether it is effectively sterile.

(b) Whether each of the following exchange/transport mechanisms is physically and geochemically viable as a pathway for delivering organics, minerals, and nutrients into this ocean, and quantify the corresponding mass and chemical fluxes:
  (i) core–ocean exchange (chemical interaction between the rocky/hydrated silicate core and the overlying liquid-water ocean);
  (ii) surface–ocean exchange (transport of surface organics and other species through the outer ice shell into the ocean);
  (iii) solid-state convection within the outer ice-Ih crust;
  (iv) transport through any intervening high-pressure ice layer separating the ocean from the rocky core.

Provide criteria, in terms of interior structure, thermal state, rheology of ice phases, and chemical partitioning, under which each pathway is or is not active, and identify which combinations of active pathways suffice to sustain a habitable ocean over geologic timescales.
